    "stem": "Chetan works with whole numbers that leave remainder $2$ when divided by $6$. He writes them as $6k+2$ with whole number $k$, and keeps the remainder strictly between $0$ and $5$.\n\nWhich two claims are correct?",
    "item_type": "mcq",
    "options": {
      "A": "Any form $6k+r$ is fine even when $r$ is $6$ or larger, because the $6k$ part is present.",
      "B": "Numbers of that kind are $6k+2$, and the remainder stays less than the divisor $6$.",
      "C": "The same numbers can be written $6(k+1)-4$, using the matching deficit below the next multiple of $6$.",
      "D": "The divisor $6$ is produced by the remainder $2$, so start from $2$ and ignore the $6k$ term."
    },
